      "problem": "A fair spinner has three regions, red, blue, and green. Suppose the spinner is spun $21$ times. What is the variance of the number of times the spinner lands on blue?",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the spinner lands on blue.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=21$ trials\n\n- the probability of landing on blue is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(21,\\frac{1}{3}).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the variance of the number of times the spinner lands on blue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =21\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
